# Severity: absolute shortfall, proportional shortfall, or something else?
next: {"title":"What counts as a cost, and why does the future cost less?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/what-counts-as-a-cost-and-why-does-the.cast"}

## Absolutt prognosetap: the years you lose
voice: male
level: basic
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 100
    'y':
      - 0
      - 75
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 140
      w: 900
      h: 600
sources:
    - id: meld34
      title: Meld. St. 34 (2015–2016) Verdier i pasientens helsetjeneste — Melding om prioritering
      authors: Helse- og omsorgsdepartementet
      year: 2016
      finding: Benefit, resource use and severity as the criteria
    - id: noma_apt
      title: Retningslinjer for dokumentasjonsgrunnlag for hurtig metodevurdering av legemidler
      authors: Legemiddelverket
      year: 2018
      finding: Absolute shortfall is counted in undiscounted QALYs

    box claim_a "Age-neutral: the disease sets severity" hidden true
    box claim_b "Age-graded: the young lose more QALYs" hidden true
    mark strike_a target claim_a kind strike hidden true

Now that we have seen why severity might buy a higher price per QALY, a committee needs an actual number for it — and some argue that number belongs to the disease alone, the same at any age.
    draw claim_a

Others argue severity is about the health actually at stake, so a young patient, with more years ahead, must score higher.
    draw claim_b

    ask "What should I call you?" intro "One thing first." store name default friend

Norway's answer is absolutt prognosetap — absolute shortfall — so let us compute it, and see which claim survives.
    clear

    axes ax x_label "Age at diagnosis (years)" y_label "Remaining QALYs (undiscounted)"

The blue curve is the age-matched healthy expectation: the remaining QALYs a Norwegian of each age can expect, quality-adjusted but, by Norwegian rule, not discounted.
    curve healthy_curve #2f6b8f expr 60*exp(-0.03358*(x-20)) x_from 15 x_to 95
    label label_healthy "Healthy expectation (age-matched)" above-right #2f6b8f attach_to healthy_curve

The red line is the prognosis with today's care for our illness — about three poor years, worth roughly one and a half QALYs, whenever it strikes.
    curve prognosis_curve #b5482e expr "1.5" x_from 15 x_to 95
    text label_prog "Prognosis with current care" #b5482e at.data [48,5] font_size 22

    pause 0.3

A twenty-year-old diagnosed today: about sixty QALYs ahead in health, one and a half with the disease.
    dot p20_h #2f6b8f at.x 20 at.y 60 guides true
    dot p20_d #b5482e at.x 20 at.y 1.5
    parallel true

Absolute shortfall is nothing but that subtraction — about fifty-eight and a half QALYs lost.
    arrow gap20 #8a5fa8 from.x 20 from.y 1.5 to.x 20 to.y 60
    label label_gap20 "APT ≈ 58.5 QALYs" right #8a5fa8 attach_to gap20 cites ["noma_apt"]

    pause 0.5

Now the same illness at eighty, where the healthy curve has already fallen to about eight QALYs.
    dot p80_h #2f6b8f at.x 80 at.y 8 guides true
    dot p80_d #b5482e at.x 80 at.y 1.5
    parallel true

Same disease, same prognosis, {name} — but the shortfall is six and a half.
    arrow gap80 #8a5fa8 from.x 80 from.y 1.5 to.x 80 to.y 8
    text label_gap80 "APT ≈ 6.5 QALYs" #8a5fa8 at.data [80,22] cites ["noma_apt"] font_size 24

Nine times the loss for an identical illness, purely because of when in a life it arrives.
    point at.ref gap20 gesture underline

Norway leaves this number undiscounted; discount it at four percent, as the cost-effectiveness model does, and the young patient's expectation would shrink from some seven times the old patient's to about three.
    highlight healthy_curve effect glow

Norway sorts this number into severity classes, and the top band — a shortfall of twenty QALYs and above — accepts roughly three times the base price per QALY.
    highlight label_gap20 effect glow

    clear

Which settles our two claims.
    show claim_a claim_b

As a description of absolute shortfall, the first is simply false: the metric counts QALYs, and an eighty-year-old has fewer of them to lose.
    draw strike_a delivery grave

That is precisely the complaint waiting in part three — nusinersen's infants sit at the very top of this scale, while a fatal illness at eighty can never reach it.
    highlight claim_b effect pulse

Absolute shortfall measures the size of the loss, never the share of a life lost — which is why the same disease counts as more severe at twenty than at eighty.

## Proportional shortfall and the fairness fight
voice: male
level: basic
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: Severity measure
    col_label: Patient
    row_values:
      - Absolute shortfall
      - Proportional shortfall
    col_values:
      - 20-year-old
      - 80-year-old
    cells:
      - [48 QALYs, 8 QALYs]
      - [80%, 100%]
    cell_notes:
      - [60 − 12, 8 − 0]
      - [48 / 60, 8 / 8]
    highlight:
      - [0, 0]
      - [1, 1]
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 120
      w: 880
      h: 560
sources:
    - id: magnussen2015
      title: På ramme alvor — alvorlighet og prioritering (the Magnussen report)
      authors: Magnussen et al.
      year: 2015
      finding: Recommends absolute shortfall as the severity measure
    - id: meld34
      title: Meld. St. 34 (2015–2016) Verdier i pasientens helsetjeneste — Melding om prioritering
      authors: Helse- og omsorgsdepartementet
      year: 2016
      finding: Adopts severity as a criterion, measured by absolute shortfall

Now that we have seen absolute shortfall count the years a patient loses, meet its rival — and whichever measure a system picks decides which patient's drug is allowed to cost more.
    text claim_no "Norway: years lost" #b5482e cites ["magnussen2015","meld34"] x 250 y 715 font_size 22
    text claim_nl "Netherlands: share lost" #2f6b8f x 750 y 715 font_size 22
    parallel true

Norway counts the years lost; the Dutch system counts the share of remaining life lost.
    draw grid row_title col_title

Two patients: a twenty-year-old with spinal muscular atrophy, and an eighty-year-old with a rapidly fatal cancer.
    draw col_header_0 col_header_1

    ask "Which of them strikes you as the more severe case?" store pick default "the 20-year-old"
        intro: Before any arithmetic, a gut check.

The first rule is Norway's: absolutt prognosetap, absolute shortfall — the quality-adjusted life years the disease takes away.
    draw row_header_0

Without the disease the twenty-year-old had about sixty QALYs ahead; with it, twelve. Forty-eight lost.
    draw cell_0_0

The eighty-year-old had eight ahead, and his cancer takes essentially all of them: eight lost.
    draw cell_0_1

You said {pick}. On this rule there is no contest: forty-eight against eight.
    draw hl_0_0

    pause 0.4

The second rule asks a different question: not how many years, but what fraction of what you had left.
    draw row_header_1

Forty-eight out of sixty — the young patient loses eighty percent of her expected health.
    draw cell_1_0

Eight out of eight — the older patient loses everything he had left.
    draw cell_1_1

And the ranking flips: on the proportional rule the eighty-year-old is the more severe case, sitting at the ceiling of one hundred percent.
    draw hl_1_1 delivery grave

    pause 0.5

Which invites a tempting conclusion: that the proportional measure has taken age out of the picture.
    text myth "Proportional shortfall takes age out of it" x 500 y 58 font_size 22

It hasn't. It only reverses the tilt — nobody can lose more than everything, so the young patient's far larger loss is squeezed against that ceiling.
    mark strike_myth target myth kind strike

The Dutch system weights severity exactly this way, by proportional shortfall.
    focus row_header_1 cell_1_1

Norway examined both — the Magnussen committee in 2015 — and the 2016 white paper chose the absolute version. Each camp accuses the other of ageism.
    focus row_header_0 cell_0_0

There is no age-neutral severity measure here: one rule pays for the years ahead of you, the other for the fraction you have left, and you can only choose which way the tilt runs.

